arbiter_feedback.description:
The regex `[^\S
]+` on line 27 also matches Unicode whitespace characters (e.g. non-breaking space U+00A0, em-space U+2003, ideographic space U+3000) because `\S` in Python's `re` module is not Unicode-aware by default for ASCII patterns — actually in Python 3, `re` IS Unicode-aware by default, so `\S` matches any Unicode non-whitespace and `\W` covers Unicode spaces. The implication is that `[^\S
]` will match Unicode whitespace, collapsing it to a space. Whether this is desired is unspecified. The `strip(' ')` on line 31 only strips ASCII space, so a line beginning or ending with a Unicode whitespace character (other than those matched by `[^\S
]+`) may not be fully stripped.
